 about colleagues.\n\nCandidate C:\nStrengths: can make correct decisions quickly, puts concern for others above everything, excellent attention skills, regarded as 100% reliable, creates a positive atmosphere with his crew.\nWeaknesses: difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not very willing to further his education.\n\nCandidate D:\nStrengths: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously.\nWeaknesses: considered moody, regarded as a loner, regarded as arrogant, relatively weak leadership skills, hot temper, know-it-all.\n\nNow evaluate criteria:\n\nReliability & responsibility: C (100% reliable), D (takes responsibility seriously), B (very conscientious), A (leadership, anticipation). So C high.\n\nTechnical & cognitive competence: A (spatial vision, complex connections), B (weather assessment, computer), C (quick correct decisions, attention), D (problem solving, concentration). All decent.\n\nCalmness, judgment, performance under pressure: B handles stress well. D responds adequately to unexpected events (some indication). A unknown. C unknown.\n\nSound and timely decision-making: C quick correct decisions, A anticipates dangerous situations, D solves problems extremely well, B good weather assessment.\n\nTeamwork, communication, crew cooperation: C creates positive atmosphere, puts concern for others, but difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric. B has interpersonal issues (grumpy, nasty remarks). A unfriendly, show-off, not open to ideas. D arrogant, weak leadership, hot temper, loner.\n\nProfessional development & long-term suitability: B has computer skills, but no mention of further education. C not willing to further education. D unknown. A unknown.\n\nThus tradeoffs: B strongest stress handling, but interpersonal issues. C strongest reliability and crew atmosphere but communication difficulty and lack of development. D strong responsibility and problem solving but poor teamwork. A strong leadership and anticipation but poor interpersonal and health.\n\nLong-distance flights require stress tolerance, reliability, decision-making, and crew cooperation. Stress handling is crucial; only B explicitly handles stress well. Crew cooperation also crucial; B's interpersonal issues could be problematic, but C's communication difficulty also problematic. Which is more severe? Hard to know.\n\nGiven the instruction to weigh seriousness for safe long-distance flight operations, stress handling may be more critical. Interpersonal issues can be mitigated with training, but inability to handle stress could be fatal. So B likely best.\n\nBut we should consider that B's interpersonal issues are multiple (grumpy, uncooperative, nasty remarks). That could degrade crew performance, especially on long flights. C's communication difficulty and egocentric may also degrade. D's arrogance and weak leadership also problematic.\n\nThus B still seems best overall.\n\nWe need to produce public message summarizing key evidence, alternatives, tradeoffs, uncertainties, what would change vote.\n\nWe also need to ask if any other agents have info on stress handling for A or D, or teamwork strengths for C beyond what we have. But we have already asked earlier and got no new info. However we can still ask targeted question to other agents using tool calls. The instruction says we can ask other agents for missing info. We still lack stress handling for A and D, and any positive teamwork strengths for C beyond what we have. We could ask Agent 1 or Agent 2 for that. But we have already asked Agent 1 earlier and they said no additional info. Agent 2 also said no. So we have exhausted. So we can proceed with provisional preference.\n\nThus produce final output."}
